  {
   "ply": 4,
   "t": 64,
   "fen": "r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/4p3/4P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 2 3",
   "line": [
    "Nc6"
   ],
   "spoken": "The recommended weapons against the double king-pawn are the Vienna and the Four Knights Scotch. The Four Knights sounds like the most boring, mundane development there is — but it's an incredibly dangerous weapon at every level under 2000, and fully legitimate above it too.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": [
    "repertoire choice"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 5,
   "t": 94.5,
   "fen": "r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/4p3/4P3/2N2N2/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKB1R b KQkq - 3 3",
   "line": [
    "Nc3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 6,
   "t": 102.5,
   "fen": "r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/3nP3/2N2N2/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKB1R w KQkq - 4 4",
   "line": [
    "Nd4"
   ],
   "spoken": "The knight jumps to d4 — a move designed to scare a beginner: 'a knight is charging into the centre!' Look at it calmly and you'll see it just wanders in with nothing behind it. It might be some trick gambit, but we don't buy the bluff.",
   "teaches": [
    "not fearing a lone central knight"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 7,
   "t": 138.5,
   "fen": "r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4N3/3nP3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKB1R b KQkq - 0 4",
   "line": [
    "Nxe5"
   ],
   "spoken": "If you were nervous you could ignore it entirely and keep developing with the bishop to c4. But there's nothing to calculate: we simply capture on e5, because the other side has only one piece out — a real trick can't exist when your opponent hasn't developed.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": [
    "no tactics without development"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 8,
   "t": 152,
   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/pppp1ppp/5n2/4N3/3nP3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKB1R w KQkq - 1 5",
   "line": [
    "Nf6"
   ],
   "spoken": "We're not satisfied with just the pawn. The Soviet-schoolboy move is to retreat the knight to f3 and smoke the intruder out of d4. Grabbing with the bishop to c4 instead invites a tricky queen-to-e7 line where White is actually much better but there's real theory to know. The point to hold onto: a knight sitting in the centre isn't automatically good — this one lacks an anchor and is easy to harass.",
   "teaches": [
    "central piece needs an anchor"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 9,
   "t": 239,
   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/pppp1ppp/5n2/8/3nP3/2N2N2/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKB1R b KQkq - 2 5",
   "line": [
    "Nf3"
   ],
   "spoken": "The real threat is the queen coming to e7 — it would chase the knight back and let Black regain the pawn on e4 at the same time. So we double back to f3, killing two birds: the knight retreats to safety.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": [
    "double-purpose retreat"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 10,
   "t": 244.5,
   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/5n2/3p4/3nP3/2N2N2/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKB1R w KQkq - 0 6",
   "line": [
    "d5"
   ],
   "spoken": "And this also contests the knight sitting on d4.",
   "kind": "main"
  },
  {
   "ply": 11,
   "t": 249.5,
   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/5n2/3p4/3NP3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKB1R b KQkq - 0 6",
   "line": [
    "Nxd4"
   ],
   "spoken": "The opponent obligingly captures on d4 and hangs the piece in one move. The game is effectively already over.",
   "teaches": [
    "punishing the blunder"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 12,
   "t": 266.5,
   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/5n2/8/3Np3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKB1R w KQkq - 0 7",
   "line": [
    "dxe4"
   ],
   "spoken": "Now convert clinically. A beginner is tempted by the bishop check on b5, developing with tempo — but it's a mistake: it allows c6, and the Nxc6 tactic only trades two minor pieces for a rook and two pawns. Still winning, but there's no reason to give material back.",
   "teaches": [
    "not every check with tempo is good"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 13,
   "t": 335,
   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/5n2/8/4p3/1NN5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKB1R b KQkq - 1 7",
   "line": [
    "Nb3"
   ],
   "spoken": "By process of elimination the knight drops back to b3: b5 walks into a6, e2 blocks the bishop — b3 keeps everything flowing so we can develop rapidly and actively.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": [
    "process of elimination"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 15,
   "t": 388,
   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/pp3ppp/5n2/2p5/4p3/1NNP4/PPP2PPP/R1BQKB1R b KQkq - 0 8",
   "line": [
    "c5",
    "d3"
   ],
   "spoken": "The pawn on e4 is annoying, hanging over the position and cramping us. Playing d3 liquidates it, makes the minor pieces easier to coordinate, and opens the dark-squared bishop toward an eventual post on g5.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": [
    "liquidate the awkward pawn"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 16,
   "t": 408.5,
   "fen": "r1b1kb1r/pp2qppp/5n2/2p5/4p3/1NNP4/PPP2PPP/R1BQKB1R w KQkq - 1 9",
   "line": [
    "Qe7"
   ],
   "spoken": "The queen-and-king alignment on the e-file is a liability, not a strength: if we seize the d-file the king has no squares, and since the c5-pawn is already committed, a future bishop check on b5 can no longer be met by c6. That's the move this is quietly setting up.",
   "teaches": [
    "king-queen alignment as a target"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 17,
   "t": 483.5,
   "fen": "r1b1kb1r/pp2qppp/5n2/2p5/4P3/1NN5/PPP2PPP/R1BQKB1R b KQkq - 0 9",
   "line": [
    "dxe4"
   ],
   "spoken": "We take back on e4 to control the d-file and keep the b5-check threat alive. The knight-takes discovery looks scary but it's a paper tiger — even without the winning check, we'd simply take and force a queen trade.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": [
    "seeing through a fake discovery"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 18,
   "t": 503,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/pp2qppp/5n2/2p2b2/4P3/1NN5/PPP2PPP/R1BQKB1R w KQkq - 1 10",
   "line": [
    "Bf5"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 19,
   "t": 520.5,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/pp2qppp/5n2/1Bp2b2/4P3/1NN5/PPP2PPP/R1BQK2R b KQkq - 2 10",
   "line": [
    "Bb5+"
   ],
   "spoken": "The bishop is untouchable — the e4-pawn is pinned to the queen — so the bishop check on b5 just develops with tempo, and then we castle.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": [
    "develop with a real tempo"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 20,
   "t": 539.5,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/pp1bqppp/5n2/1Bp5/4P3/1NN5/PPP2PPP/R1BQK2R w KQkq - 3 11",
   "line": [
    "Bd7"
   ],
   "spoken": "When you're up a piece there are usually four or five roughly equal moves in every position; defending e4 with f3 is a perfectly fine working move. The bishop check is just a touch more to the point.",
   "teaches": [
    "many roads when winning"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 21,
   "t": 571.5,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/pp1Bqppp/5n2/2p5/4P3/1NN5/PPP2PPP/R1BQK2R b KQkq - 0 11",
   "line": [
    "Bxd7+"
   ],
   "spoken": "We trade the bishops off and, crucially, force Black to recapture with the knight — clearing the way to plant our own knight on a beautiful d5 stronghold.",
   "kind": "plan",
   "teaches": [
    "trade to win a stronghold"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 22,
   "t": 590,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/pp1q1ppp/5n2/2p5/4P3/1NN5/PPP2PPP/R1BQK2R w KQkq - 0 12",
   "line": [
    "Qxd7"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 23,
   "t": 592,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/pp1Q1ppp/5n2/2p5/4P3/1NN5/PPP2PPP/R1B1K2R b KQkq - 0 12",
   "line": [
    "Qxd7+"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 24,
   "t": 595.5,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/pp1n1ppp/8/2p5/4P3/1NN5/PPP2PPP/R1B1K2R w KQkq - 0 13",
   "line": [
    "Nxd7"
   ],
   "spoken": "Queens come off, but a queen trade is no licence to abandon principle — development still comes first. Castling queenside is the plan here: no risk left, and it drops the rook straight onto the open d-file.",
   "kind": "plan",
   "teaches": [
    "principles survive the queen trade"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 25,
   "t": 650,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/pp1n1ppp/8/2p5/4PB2/1NN5/PPP2PPP/R3K2R b KQkq - 1 13",
   "line": [
    "Bf4"
   ],
   "spoken": "The bishop to g5 would walk into f6 or h6, so it goes to f4 instead — invulnerable there, and covering d6 to make it harder for Black to untangle.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": [
    "find the safe active square"
   ]
  },
  {
